      "S2",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 52,
    "html": "<p>Bobby G. Stevenson is an American technology entrepreneur who co-founded Ciber, the computer consulting business. A former programmer analyst at IBM and LTV Steel, he led Ciber as chief executive for more than two decades. His ownership in the company created his fortune as it expanded through acquisitions and broader technical services.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "When Ciber co-founder Bobby G. Stevenson gave $7 million to Texas Tech's business college, he dedicated the gift to his father. His father had raised him in rural West Texas, paid for college and lent him money to start his business. Stevenson wanted rural students to receive similar opportunities.\n\nHe initially offered funds for scholarships. Chancellor John Montford asked him to add an endowed professorship to the gift, and Stevenson agreed. His priorities were practical: he wanted students to develop clear writing and basic arithmetic, skills he had sometimes found wanting among employees. He later became an executive lecturer at the university.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary university reporting details the donation and motivation; a separate corporate release confirms the later teaching role."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S1"
    ],
    "word_count": 102,
    "html": "<p>When Ciber co-founder Bobby G. Stevenson gave $7 million to Texas Tech's business college, he dedicated the gift to his father. His father had raised him in rural West Texas, paid for college and lent him money to start his business. Stevenson wanted rural students to receive similar opportunities.</p>\r\n<p>He initially offered funds for scholarships. Chancellor John Montford asked him to add an endowed professorship to the gift, and Stevenson agreed. His priorities were practical: he wanted students to develop clear writing and basic arithmetic, skills he had sometimes found wanting among employees. He later became an executive lecturer at the university.</p>\r\n"
  },
